        "parts" : [ {
          "name" : "overview",
          "prose" : "# Context\n\nThis section describes security controls applicable to AI application and AI model development and extends the ‘Software development fundamentals’ section of these guidelines.\n\n# Further information\n\nFurther information on conversational AI application security risks can be found in the [OWASP Top 10 for LLM Applications 2025](#890d202b-ec79-4ea7-b0df-0cf7d7b7ad29) publication.\n\nFurther information on agentic AI application security risks can be found in the [OWASP Top 10 For Agentic Applications 2026](#9fdb8571-d69e-4fc8-8b6c-1825e48eb340) publication.\n\nFurther information on AI security risks can be found in the following ASD publications:\n\n- [An introduction to artificial intelligence](#e513ac6b-09fa-4d53-951a-a5142208cf12)\n- [Artificial intelligence and machine learning: Supply chain risks and mitigations](#233c7982-dc01-496e-b761-b24ac06ded49)\n- [Engaging with artificial intelligence](#0248ace8-2e08-40f8-b19e-117c76909cf1).\n\nFurther information on AI security risks can also be found in the following publications:\n\n- MITRE’s [Adversarial Threat Landscape for Artificial-Intelligence Systems](#1258a1ce-774a-4701-8d47-80940b988a2d)\n- National Institute of Standards and Technology AI 100-2 E2023, [Adversarial Machine Learning: A Taxonomy and Terminology of Attacks and Mitigations](#9af7c141-51de-43f0-97bc-547e24e96545)\n- United Kingdom’s National Cyber Security Centre and United States’ Cybersecurity \\& Infrastructure Security Agency’s [Guidelines for secure AI system development](#353dcc1a-23e6-48d5-8047-950cc614bf0b)\n- United States’ National Security Agency’s [AI Data Security: Best Practices for Securing Data Used to Train \\& Operate AI Systems](#6e17dd11-a1c5-4f2b-87df-c5edde5fc8bd)\n- United States’ National Security Agency’s [Deploying AI Systems Securely: Best Practices for Deploying Secure and Resilient AI Systems](#c5ca2027-161e-4322-9367-7ec736b4846f)."
        } ],
