          "title" : "802.1X authentication and key exchange",
          "props" : [ {
            "name" : "sort-id",
            "value" : "catalog[1].group[22].group[1].group[10]"
          } ],
          "parts" : [ {
            "name" : "overview",
            "prose" : "802.1X authentication requires the use of an Extensible Authentication Protocol (EAP), of which multiple EAP methods are available.\n\nExtensible Authentication Protocol-Transport Layer Security (EAP-TLS) is considered one of the most secure EAP methods for 802.1X authentication and is widely supported. It uses a public key infrastructure to secure communications between devices and a Remote Authentication Dial-In User Service (RADIUS) server by using X.509 certificates. While EAP-TLS provides strong mutual authentication, it requires an organisation to have established a public key infrastructure. This involves deploying their own certificate authority and issuing X.509 certificates, or sourcing them from a commercial certificate authority, for every device that accesses their network. While this introduces additional costs and management overheads, the security advantages are significant."
          } ],
