        "title" : "Cross domain solutions",
        "props" : [ {
          "name" : "sort-id",
          "value" : "catalog[1].group[24].group[2]"
        } ],
        "parts" : [ {
          "name" : "overview",
          "prose" : "# Context\n\nA cross domain solution (CDS) is a system comprised of security-enforcing functions tailored to mitigate specific security risks associated with accessing or transferring data between different security domains. CDSs may be an integrated appliance or, more commonly, composed of discrete technologies or sub-systems, with each sub-system consisting of hardware or software components.\n\nThis section defines two types of CDSs, Transfer CDSs and Access CDSs. Transfer CDSs facilitate the transfer of data in one direction (unidirectional) or multiple directions (bi-directional) between different security domains. In comparison, Access CDSs provide users with access to multiple security domains from a single device; however, they do not allow users to move data between the different security domains.\n\nThis section describes security controls applicable to CDSs and extends the ‘Gateways’ section of these guidelines. Additional sections of these guidelines should also be consulted depending on the types of CDSs being deployed.\n\nPersonnel involved in the planning, design, implementation or assessment of CDSs should also refer to ASD’s [Introduction to Cross Domain Solutions](#86847be4-3a06-4d2f-9952-447158593e0d) and [Fundamentals of Cross Domain Solutions](#f32020e9-4cb3-486b-bcbb-6fb6719f58a5) publications.\n\n# Further information\n\nFurther information on cyber supply chain risk management can be found in the ‘Cyber supply chain risk management’ section of the [Guidelines for procurement and outsourcing](#f37a4848-0791-4870-b316-5536c2681c28).\n\nFurther information on evaluated products can be found in the ‘Evaluated product procurement’ section of the [Guidelines for evaluated products](#a699a3aa-828d-479b-b50b-98127bb19437).\n\nFurther information on designing, configuring and managing networks can be found in the ‘Network design and configuration’ section of the [Guidelines for networking](#f145ff5b-d396-4248-8f48-621349d6f0ed).\n\nFurther information on event logging can be found in the ‘Security monitoring’ section of the [Guidelines for security assurance](#ef60c0e4-acfb-4a8b-b5ed-25675c6e117c).\n\nFurther information on event logging for network devices in CDSs can also be found in ASD’s [Priority logs for SIEM ingestion: Practitioner guidance](#1dbda98a-4e8b-4a52-b4f7-9d1a895fd324) publication.\n\nFurther information on cyber security awareness training can be found in the ‘Cyber security awareness training’ section of the [Guidelines for personnel security](#7d16ae67-87a7-4861-b939-e13ec279b5a2)."
        } ],
        "groups" : [ {
          "title" : "Implementing cross domain solutions",
          "props" : [ {
